# Copy to .env.prod on the Lightsail box (at /home/ubuntu/geneav/.env.prod),
# set the values below, then let CI deploy — or by hand:
#   docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --no-build
#
# GENEAV_HOST is the public hostname Caddy serves (and obtains a Let's Encrypt
# certificate for). It also becomes the frontend's build-time API base URL and the
# backend's allowed CORS origin, so a domain change requires a rebuild.
#
# Production uses a registered domain whose A record points at the Lightsail
# STATIC IP (e.g. geneav.com -> 1.2.3.4). www is redirected to the apex in
# caddy/Caddyfile, and analytics.geneav.com needs its own A record at the same
# address. The IP is free while attached to a running instance, and billed if
# you leave it allocated but detached.
#
# No registered domain, or cutting over in stages? Use a free nip.io wildcard
# derived from the IP instead — this is the recommended way to verify a fresh
# box before touching production DNS:
#   IP 1.2.3.4  ->  GENEAV_HOST=geneav.1-2-3-4.nip.io   (no DNS records needed)
# If Let's Encrypt rate-limits the shared nip.io domain, use sslip.io the same way.

GENEAV_HOST=geneav.com

# The www redirect and the analytics subdomain are DERIVED from GENEAV_HOST by
# docker-compose.prod.yml (www.<host> and analytics.<host>), so a nip.io staging
# run gets names that resolve to the staging box instead of dragging geneav.com
# into it. Set these only if the real layout ever stops being "www." and
# "analytics." in front of the apex.
# GENEAV_WWW_HOST=www.geneav.com
# GENEAV_ANALYTICS_HOST=analytics.geneav.com

# Chat assistant (OpenAI). Leave empty to keep chat disabled. Put the REAL key
# only in .env.prod (gitignored) on the box — never commit it to this example.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# Transactional email (signup acknowledgement; password reset).
#
# GENEAV_MAIL_ENABLED=true ALONE DOES NOT SEND MAIL. Without a reachable
# SMTP_HOST the send fails and logs a warning on every signup. Set the three
# SMTP_* values below at the same time, or leave GENEAV_MAIL_ENABLED=false.
#
# Provider: Hostinger email. Get the credentials by:
#   1. hPanel -> Emails -> create a mailbox on geneav.com (e.g. no-reply@geneav.com).
#   2. Add the MX + SPF/DKIM records Hostinger shows to the registrar's DNS for
#      geneav.com. Until they propagate, mail may be rejected or land in spam.
#   3. The mailbox address is the SMTP_USERNAME; its mailbox password is the
#      SMTP_PASSWORD (there is no separate API key).
#
# Put the REAL password only in .env.prod (gitignored, root-owned) on the box —
# never here. Keep port 587: AWS, like Azure before it, throttles outbound port
# 25 by default, so 25 is not an option without asking support to lift it.
#
# GENEAV_MAIL_FROM must be a mailbox on the domain or the provider rejects it.
# Without the DKIM/SPF records, password-reset mail lands in spam.
GENEAV_MAIL_ENABLED=true
SMTP_HOST=smtp.hostinger.com
SMTP_USERNAME=no-reply@geneav.com
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_PORT=587                        # 587 = STARTTLS (the default); 465 = implicit TLS
# GENEAV_MAIL_FROM=no-reply@geneav.com
# GENEAV_MAIL_FROM_NAME=geneav
# GENEAV_MAIL_LOGIN_URL is set automatically from GENEAV_HOST by the prod compose.

# ECR pull credentials — NORMALLY LEAVE THESE UNSET.
#
# Deploys pull images rather than building on the box. The box is an SSM hybrid
# managed node, so the agent keeps refreshed credentials for its activation role
# (geneav-ssm-instance) on disk, and `aws ecr get-login-password` just works with
# nothing stored here. That role is scoped PULL-ONLY to the three repositories, so
# a compromise of the box cannot push a poisoned image.
#
# CONFIRMED ON THE BOX, 2 Aug 2026: the agent resolves to
# assumed-role/geneav-ssm-instance/mi-..., `docker login` against ECR succeeds,
# and ecr:PutImage is refused with AccessDenied. So there is no registry
# credential on this host at all — a strict improvement on the Azure ACR token
# this replaced, which did sit in this file.
#
# The variables below are a fallback for the case where that credential refresh
# is unavailable. If you need them, create a dedicated IAM user with only
# ecr:GetAuthorizationToken plus BatchGetImage / GetDownloadUrlForLayer /
# BatchCheckLayerAvailability on geneav-backend and geneav-frontend — never an
# admin key, and never the CI push role.
# ECR_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# ECR_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Region the box talks to AWS in. Used for the ECR login and the backup upload.
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Off-box database backups. The postgres-backup sidecar dumps nightly to a local
# Docker volume AND copies each dump here. Leave BACKUP_S3_BUCKET empty and it
# reverts to local-only dumps — which do NOT survive losing the instance, so
# set it in production.
#
# The credentials belong to the geneav-backup-writer IAM user created by
# aws-provision.sh. That user can ONLY s3:PutObject: it cannot list, read or
# delete, so a compromised box cannot shred the backups it has already written.
#
# Retention in the bucket is a 14-day lifecycle rule, not the sidecar's job.
# 14 days is what the privacy policy promises users about deleted records
# lingering in backups — do not raise either number without the other.
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
BACKUP_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14              # local volume retention only

# PostgreSQL (accounts, API keys, usage). REQUIRED in prod — the prod compose
# refuses to start without it. Use a strong, unique value; data persists in the
# postgres-data Docker volume across redeploys.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# POSTGRES_DB=geneav
# POSTGRES_USER=geneav

# Demo API key for the website's own try-it features.
#
# /api/v1/scan and /api/v1/chat require an API key from every caller, so the
# homepage scanner and the chat widget — used by visitors with no account — go
# through server-side routes under /site-api/* that attach this key. It stays in
# the frontend container and is never sent to a browser.
#
# Mint it on the box AFTER the stack is up, against the real database:
#   curl -sX POST https://<GENEAV_HOST>/api/v1/signup \
#        -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"email":"demo@<GENEAV_HOST>"}'
# Copy the returned "apiKey" here (it is shown exactly once) and restart the
# frontend. Read at runtime, so rotating it needs no image rebuild.
#
# Leaving it empty is safe and is the correct state on a fresh box: the demo
# answers 503 with a "sign up for a key" message and nothing else changes. The
# demo account's plan quota is what caps demo usage — keep it on a free tier.
GENEAV_DEMO_API_KEY=

# Microsoft Marketplace billing (paid plans; Microsoft is the merchant of record).
#
# Disabled by default: with GENEAV_MARKETPLACE_ENABLED=false every
# /api/v1/marketplace endpoint returns 503 and the site is otherwise unchanged.
# Deploy with it false first, confirm nothing moved, then enable.
#
# These come from the SINGLE-TENANT fulfillment app registration (App B in
# docs/marketplace-plan.md) — NOT the multi-tenant sign-in app below. The same
# tenant + client id must be entered in the offer's Partner Center Technical
# Configuration page, because they are what the webhook JWT is validated against.
# Put the REAL secret only in .env.prod (gitignored, root-owned) on the box.
GENEAV_MARKETPLACE_ENABLED=false
MARKETPLACE_TENANT_ID=
MARKETPLACE_CLIENT_ID=
MARKETPLACE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Offer identity. Both are PERMANENT once the offer is published, and
# MARKETPLACE_OFFER_ID must match the offer id in Partner Center. The plan ids
# (geneav-starter / geneav-pro / geneav-scale) map to tiers in application.yml
# under geneav.marketplace.plan-map — keep the two in step.
MARKETPLACE_PUBLISHER_ID=
# MARKETPLACE_OFFER_ID=geneav-scan-api

# "Sign in with Microsoft" (Entra ID OIDC). Required for the marketplace landing
# page — Microsoft's certification checks that buyers can sign in with Entra.
#
# These come from the MULTI-TENANT sign-in app (App A), so buyers from any Entra
# organisation can sign in. Redirect URI to register:
#   https://<GENEAV_HOST>/login/oauth2/code/microsoft
# Delegated permission User.Read only — never anything marked "needs admin
# consent", which would block every non-admin buyer from the landing page.
GENEAV_MICROSOFT_LOGIN_ENABLED=false
MICROSOFT_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_LOGIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GENEAV_MICROSOFT_POST_LOGIN_URL is set automatically from GENEAV_HOST by the
# prod compose. The frontend's matching build-time flag is derived from
# GENEAV_MICROSOFT_LOGIN_ENABLED above — but it is baked in at BUILD time, so
# flipping it requires a redeploy, not just a restart.

# Analytics (self-hosted Umami, served at https://analytics.geneav.com).
#
# On its own subdomain because Umami bakes its base path in at build time and
# serves collection at the root, which under the apex would collide with /api/*.
# Needs an A record for `analytics` pointing at the Lightsail static IP; Caddy issues the cert.
#
# UMAMI_APP_SECRET signs the Umami login session. REQUIRED — the prod compose
# refuses to start without it. Any long random string; changing it later logs
# you out of the Umami dashboard but loses no analytics data. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
UMAMI_APP_SECRET=

# ⚠️ MOVED TO CI (27 July 2026). NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the
# client bundle when the frontend IMAGE is built, and images are now built by
# GitHub Actions rather than on the box — so setting this here has no effect.
# It lives as the repository variable NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID instead
# (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables). Not a secret: it
# ships to every browser that loads the site.
#
# The variable below is kept only as a record of what the old box carried.
#
# ⚠️ THE AWS MOVE STARTED FROM AN EMPTY DATABASE, so Umami issued a NEW website
# id. Analytics history from the Azure box did not come across. Re-run these
# steps against the new box and put the result in the CI variable:
#   1. Deploy with the variable EMPTY. No analytics tag is rendered; the site is
#      otherwise unaffected.
#   2. Open https://analytics.geneav.com, sign in (admin / umami) and CHANGE THE PASSWORD.
#   3. Settings -> Websites -> Add website. Copy the id it generates.
#   4. Set the repository variable and re-run the build — the id is baked into
#      the frontend bundle at BUILD time, so a restart alone will not pick it up.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=   (repository variable, not read from this file)
